# ---- Data and artifacts: never commit these ----
# MedMNIST downloads (DermaMNIST .npz files). Public dataset, but 10s of MB and
# re-downloadable; committing data makes the repo fat and unreproducible.
data/

# Trained checkpoints. Reproducible from train_dermamnist.py + a recorded seed.
checkpoints/

# Benchmark, quantisation and comparison output. These ARE the deliverable
# numbers, so the JSON records themselves are committed: every figure quoted in
# README.md can then be traced back to the record that produced it, instead of
# the README citing files a reader cannot open.
#
# `results/*` plus a negation is required here. Git cannot re-include a file
# whose parent directory is excluded, so a plain `results/` rule means nothing
# inside it can ever be committed -- which is how the README ended up
# referencing eight result files that would have 404'd on GitHub.
# Large artefacts (onnxruntime profiler dumps, captured stderr) stay ignored.
results/*
!results/*.json

# ONNX exports and quantised models
*.onnx
*.engine
*.trt

# ---- Python ----
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*.egg-info/
.pytest_cache/
.coverage
build/
dist/

# ---- Environments ----
.venv/
venv/
.env

# ---- Scratch ----
*.log
scratch/

# ---- Private, not part of the published project ----
# LEARNING-NOTES.md is a personal study log: question sequences, corrections of
# my own wrong explanations, and cross-window task lists. It is deliberately kept
# OUT of the published repository while staying on disk at the same path, so the
# study workflow is unaffected. A published repo should carry engineering
# evidence, not preparation notes.
LEARNING-NOTES.md
